      <section class="tg-section" id="turn">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Question, and the Turn</h2>

        <p>Korah's demand was <strong>centripetal</strong>. He wanted <em>in</em> — into the
        restricted zone, past the boundary, up to the altar. Everything about the Sinai
        economy invited that desire and forbade its satisfaction: the mountain fenced, the
        people warned not to touch it on pain of death
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 19:12-13">Exodus 19:12–13</a>), a single man
        permitted into the innermost place a single day a year.</p>

        <p>The New Testament's answer has two halves, and both are surprising.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Two Moves Everything Else Follows From</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Korah wanted the door. The New Testament opens the
          door and then points through it in the opposite direction.</p>
          <p><strong>First: the boundary is removed.</strong> "Since we have confidence to
          enter the holy places by the blood of Jesus… let us draw near"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 10:19-22">Hebrews 10:19–22</a>). The verb is the
          priestly verb. The answer to Korah's question is <strong>yes</strong>.</p>
          <p><strong>Second: the direction reverses.</strong> Having been brought in, the
          assembly is immediately sent out. "Go therefore"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 28:19">Matthew 28:19</a>). "You will be my
          witnesses… to the end of the earth"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 1:8">Acts 1:8</a>). The Sinai economy pulled inward
          toward a guarded centre; the apostolic economy pushes outward from an opened one.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="church">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Church — What the New Testament Actually Builds</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">A Philological Thread Worth Following</h3>

        <p>Korah's charge (Numbers 16:3) uses two nouns: <em>'edah</em> (congregation) and
        <em>qehal YHWH</em> — <strong>the assembly of the LORD.</strong> That second phrase
        is a technical term. <a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 23">Deuteronomy 23</a> uses
        it to legislate who may and may not <em>enter</em> it: not the emasculated, not the
        illegitimate, not the Ammonite or Moabite. <em>Qahal</em> is the standard word for
        the covenant assembly considered as a bounded body with an entry question attached
        to it.</p>

        <p>The Septuagint's usual rendering of <em>qahal</em> is <strong>ἐκκλησία</strong> —
        <em>ekklesia</em>. Stephen, in <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 7:38">Acts 7:38</a>,
        calls wilderness Israel exactly that: "the <em>ekklesia</em> in the wilderness." And
        <em>ekklesia</em> is the word the New Testament chooses for the church.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">The Word From Korah's Own Sentence</span>
          <p>The New Testament names the church with the term from Korah's own sentence — the
          assembly whose membership was defined by an entry restriction — and then
          systematically dismantles the restriction.</p>
          <p>This is not wordplay. Deuteronomy 23:1 excluded the eunuch from the
          <em>qahal</em>. <a class="ref" data-ref="Isaiah 56:3-5">Isaiah 56:3–5</a> promised
          him a name better than sons and daughters. <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 8">Acts 8</a>
          delivers it, and the Ethiopian's question is the exact question Korah asked, in its
          correct form: <strong>"What prevents me from being baptised?"</strong> Philip's
          answer is: nothing.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Images the New Testament Uses</h3>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead><tr><th>Image</th><th>Texts</th><th>What it does with Korah's question</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Temple</strong></td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 3:16-17">1 Cor 3:16–17</a>; 6:19; <a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 2:19-22">Eph 2:19–22</a></td><td>The restricted zone is now the assembly itself, and the individual believer. The sanctuary has no perimeter left to breach</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Priesthood</strong></td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 2:5">1 Pet 2:5</a>, 9; <a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 1:6">Rev 1:6</a>; 5:10</td><td>Korah's conclusion granted — to the whole body, on other grounds</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Body</strong></td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 12">1 Cor 12</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 12:4-8">Rom 12:4–8</a></td><td>Differentiation without gradation of worth: 1 Cor 12:22–24 inverts honour toward the "less honourable" parts</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Household</strong></td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 2:19">Eph 2:19</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 3:15">1 Tim 3:15</a></td><td>Kinship, not caste. Members by adoption (Rom 8:15; Gal 4:5)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Flock</strong></td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="John 10">John 10</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 5:2-4">1 Pet 5:2–4</a></td><td>Authority as care, explicitly <em>not</em> domineering (1 Pet 5:3)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Bride</strong></td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 5:25-32">Eph 5:25–32</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 21:2">Rev 21:2</a></td><td>The relation is covenantal love; her holiness is given, not achieved</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Building / stones</strong></td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 2:4-8">1 Pet 2:4–8</a>; Eph 2:20–22</td><td>Every member load-bearing; the cornerstone is Christ, not an order</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Note What Is Absent — and What This Does Not Settle</span>
          <strong>No New Testament image of the church is sacral-hierarchical in the Aaronide
          sense</strong> — no image of a caste holding access on behalf of a laity barred from
          it. Even the shepherd image, the closest to an authority metaphor, arrives with the
          immediate qualification <em>not domineering over those in your charge</em>
          (1 Peter 5:3), and its background text is Ezekiel 34's judgment on shepherds who feed
          themselves. This does not settle the Part III disputes about orders — a Catholic
          reading holds that the ministerial priesthood is a real participation in Christ's,
          not a survival of Aaron's, and that the temple-to-people transfer does not by itself
          exclude it. But it does establish the <em>baseline</em> on which any account of
          office has to be built.
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="priesthood">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Priesthood of All Believers — What It Is, and What It Isn't</h2>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          You yourselves like living stones are being built up as a spiritual house, to be a
          holy priesthood, to offer spiritual sacrifices acceptable to God through Jesus Christ.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 2:5">1 Peter 2:5</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Korah's Proof-Text, With Four Words Added</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Korah's premise was Exodus 19:6. Peter's premise is
          Exodus 19:6. The difference is the last four words of 1 Peter 2:5: <em>through
          Jesus Christ</em>.</p>
          <p>Korah reasoned: <em>we are holy, therefore we may approach.</em> Peter reasons:
          <em>you have been made a priesthood in Christ, therefore offer.</em> The conclusion
          is nearly identical; the ground is entirely different, and it is not in the
          worshipper. Peter then adds v. 10 — <em>once you were not a people, but now you are
          God's people; once you had not received mercy, but now you have received mercy.</em>
          The status is not an audit finding. It is a gift to those who did not have it.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">What the "Spiritual Sacrifices" Actually Are</h3>

        <p>The New Testament is concrete about the content of this priesthood, and the list is
        not what devotional usage usually suggests:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>One's own body</strong> — "present your bodies as a living sacrifice,
          holy and acceptable to God, which is your spiritual worship"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 12:1">Romans 12:1</a>). The whole of Romans 12–15
          is the exposition of that sentence, and it is almost entirely about ordinary conduct
          toward other people.</li>
          <li><strong>Praise</strong> — "a sacrifice of praise to God, that is, the fruit of
          lips that acknowledge his name" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 13:15">Hebrews 13:15</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>Doing good and sharing</strong> — "do not neglect to do good and to share
          what you have, for such sacrifices are pleasing to God"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 13:16">Hebrews 13:16</a>). Note the word:
          <em>sacrifices</em>. Generosity is priestly service.</li>
          <li><strong>Financial gifts</strong> — Epaphroditus' delivery of the Philippians'
          support is "a fragrant offering, a sacrifice acceptable and pleasing to God"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Philippians 4:18">Philippians 4:18</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>Evangelism</strong> — the strongest and least noticed. Paul calls himself
          a minister (<em>leitourgos</em>) of Christ Jesus to the Gentiles, serving as a priest
          (<em>hierourgounta</em>) the gospel of God, so that the offering
          (<em>prosphora</em>) of the Gentiles may be acceptable
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 15:16">Romans 15:16</a>). The mission itself is a
          liturgy, and the converts are the offering.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">What It Does Not Mean</span>
          <p>The priesthood of all believers is not the abolition of order, and the same
          documents that teach it also teach office.</p>
          <p><strong>Christ gave</strong> apostles, prophets, evangelists, shepherds and
          teachers — "to equip the saints for the work of ministry"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 4:11-12">Ephesians 4:11–12</a>). The offices
          exist for the priesthood's work, not in place of it.</p>
          <p><strong>Qualifications are stipulated</strong>
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 3:1-13">1 Timothy 3:1–13</a>;
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Titus 1:5-9">Titus 1:5–9</a>), and they are overwhelmingly
          about character, with a single competence requirement (able to teach) and an explicit
          warning against novices lest they be "puffed up with conceit" (1 Timothy 3:6).
          <em>That last phrase is the Korah diagnosis in a qualification list.</em></p>
          <p><strong>Leaders are to be honoured and obeyed</strong>
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Thessalonians 5:12-13">1 Thess 5:12–13</a>;
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 13:17">Hebrews 13:17</a>), and simultaneously to be
          examples rather than lords (1 Peter 5:3), and accountable — an accusation against an
          elder requires two or three witnesses, and public sin is publicly rebuked
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 5:19-20">1 Timothy 5:19–20</a>).</p>
          <p><strong>Order is commanded</strong>: "all things should be done decently and in
          order" (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 14:40">1 Corinthians 14:40</a>), in
          the middle of the most charismatically exuberant chapter in the canon.</p>
        </div>

        <p>This is exactly Luther's <em>Amt</em> / <em>Priestertum</em> distinction, and it is
        why Numbers 16 still has purchase after Hebrews 10: the veil is torn, and it remains
        true that no one takes the honour of office to himself.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="community">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Community — Access Produces Assembly</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Three "Let Us"</h3>

        <p><a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 10:19-25">Hebrews 10:19–25</a> is the single best
        answer in the New Testament to the question of what happens after the boundary falls.
        Three exhortations follow from the opened way, and the third is the surprise:</p>

        <ol>
          <li><em>Let us <strong>draw near</strong> with a true heart in full assurance of
          faith</em> (v. 22) — the vertical access, Korah's question answered.</li>
          <li><em>Let us <strong>hold fast</strong> the confession of our hope</em> (v. 23) —
          perseverance.</li>
          <li><em>Let us <strong>consider how to stir up one another</strong> to love and good
          works, <strong>not neglecting to meet together</strong></em> (vv. 24–25) — the
          horizontal assembly.</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Access Produces Assembly</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The moment the sanctuary opens, the text turns to
          attendance, mutual provocation to love, and encouragement. Private access is not the
          point of the torn veil; it makes a gathered priesthood possible.</p>
          <p>This is also why the New Testament has no category for an unattached Christian.
          The priesthood is corporate, and its sacrifices — doing good, sharing, encouraging —
          have no meaning without other people to perform them toward.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The "One Another" Corpus</h3>

        <p>There are roughly fifty reciprocal commands in the New Testament epistles. Love one
        another (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 13:34">John 13:34</a>); bear with one another
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 4:2">Ephesians 4:2</a>); forgive one another
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 4:32">Ephesians 4:32</a>); confess sins to one
        another and pray for one another (<a class="ref" data-ref="James 5:16">James 5:16</a>);
        submit to one another out of reverence for Christ
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 5:21">Ephesians 5:21</a>); bear one another's
        burdens (<a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 6:2">Galatians 6:2</a>); do not speak evil
        against one another (<a class="ref" data-ref="James 4:11">James 4:11</a>); honour one
        another above yourselves (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 12:10">Romans 12:10</a>).</p>

        <p>Almost none of these can be performed alone, and almost none of them can be
        delegated to an office. This is the practical content of the priesthood of all
        believers, and it is unglamorous.</p>

        <p>The Jerusalem summaries (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 2:42-47">Acts 2:42–47</a>;
        4:32–35) give the shape: apostolic teaching, fellowship, breaking bread, prayers, and a
        radical disposal of property toward need. Notably these are descriptive, not legislated
        — <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 5:4">Acts 5:4</a> has Peter tell Ananias the property
        and the proceeds were his own. The community's economics are a fruit, not a rule.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Anti-Korah Procedure: Matthew 18</h3>

        <p>Korah's method was to gather a coalition and confront the leadership publicly, in
        force, before any private approach.
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 18:15-20">Matthew 18:15–20</a> legislates precisely
        the reverse sequence:</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">The Reverse Sequence</span>
          <p><strong>1. Go privately.</strong> "If your brother sins against you, go and tell
          him his fault, between you and him alone."<br>
          <strong>2. Escalate minimally.</strong> Two or three witnesses, only on refusal.<br>
          <strong>3. Involve the assembly</strong> only after that fails.<br>
          <strong>4. The goal throughout is "you have gained your brother"</strong> —
          restoration, not vindication.</p>
          <p>Korah's procedure inverted every step: maximum publicity first, coalition before
          conversation, and the goal was displacement rather than restoration. The Matthew 18
          sequence is the New Testament's answer to the <em>manner</em> of dispute, which is
          exactly what <em>m. Avot</em> 5:17 identified as the fault — not that Korah disagreed,
          but how.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Grievance Handled Rightly: Acts 6</h3>

        <p><a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 6:1">Acts 6:1</a> records a real complaint from a
        minority faction against the leadership: the Hellenist widows were being neglected in
        the daily distribution. This is structurally a Korah situation — a group within the
        congregation alleging that those in charge are administering unfairly.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">What the Apostles Do Not Do</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">They do not call it rebellion. They do not invoke their
          office. They do not ask whether the complainants had standing. They concede the
          problem and restructure.</p>
          <p>The work is delegated to seven men chosen <em>by the congregation</em>, and the
          chapter ends with the word of God increasing and the disciples multiplying greatly.
          Set beside Numbers 16, this is the clearest available demonstration that a complaint
          about leadership is not by nature Korahite, and that the difference lies in what is
          sought — remedy or office. The same holds for
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 2:11-14">Galatians 2:11–14</a>, where Paul opposes
          Peter publicly and records it in Scripture. <strong>A canon that contains Acts 6 and
          Galatians 2 cannot be used to teach that questioning leadership is
          rebellion.</strong></p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Disputable Matters</h3>

        <p><a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 14">Romans 14</a>–15 and
        <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 8">1 Corinthians 8</a>–10 supply the New
        Testament's protocol for conflicts that are <em>not</em> about the gospel: the strong
        bear with the weak, no one despises or judges, each is convinced in his own mind, and
        the operative constraint is that nothing be done which causes a brother to stumble.
        Romans 14:4 — "Who are you to pass judgment on the servant of another? It is before his
        own master that he stands or falls" — is a direct application of the 2 Timothy 2:19
        principle, which is itself the Korah verse.</p>

        <p><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 1:10-17">1 Corinthians 1:10–17</a> and 3:1–9
        address the party spirit — <em>I follow Paul, I follow Apollos</em> — with the argument
        that the leaders are servants through whom you believed, and that the growth is God's.
        That is the shortest possible cure for Korah-style factionalism: it is not that the
        parties chose wrong leaders, but that leaders are not the sort of thing to form parties
        around.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="mission">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Mission — The Direction Reverses</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Moses' Wish and Korah's Grab</h3>

        <p>Five chapters before Korah's rebellion, something happens that changes how the whole
        episode should be read. In <a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 11">Numbers 11</a>, the
        Spirit that is on Moses is distributed to seventy elders, and two men — Eldad and Medad
        — prophesy in the camp rather than at the tent. Joshua wants them stopped.</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Are you jealous for my sake? Would that all the LORD's people were prophets, that the
          LORD would put his Spirit on them!
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 11:29">Numbers 11:29</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Deepest Thing Numbers 16 Has to Say</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Moses wanted what Korah wanted. Moses desired the
          universal endowment of the Spirit as a <em>gift God might give</em>. Korah demanded it
          as a <em>right he could take</em>.</p>
          <p>And Moses' wish is granted — at Pentecost.
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Joel 2:28-29">Joel 2:28–29</a>, quoted by Peter in
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 2:17-18">Acts 2:17–18</a>: <em>I will pour out my
          Spirit on all flesh; your sons and your daughters shall prophesy… even on my male
          servants and female servants.</em></p>
          <p>The democratisation Korah demanded at swordpoint is given away for free, to slaves
          and daughters, by the God who buried him for reaching. <strong>This is not a warning
          against wanting more of God. It is a warning about the difference between petition and
          seizure.</strong></p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Barrier Comes Down</h3>

        <p><a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 2:11-22">Ephesians 2:11–22</a> is the mission text
        that most directly engages the Numbers economy. Gentiles were <em>separated from Christ,
        alienated from the commonwealth of Israel, strangers to the covenants</em> — that is, on
        the wrong side of the <em>qahal</em> boundary. Christ "has broken down in his flesh the
        dividing wall of hostility" (v. 14), "so that he might create in himself one new man in
        place of the two" (v. 15).</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">The Image Was Literal</span>
          Herod's temple had a stone barrier — the <em>soreg</em> — around the inner courts,
          carrying a Greek inscription forbidding any foreigner to pass on pain of death. Two
          copies of that inscription have been recovered; Josephus describes it
          (<em>War</em> 5.193–94; <em>Ant.</em> 15.417).
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 21:28-29">Acts 21:28–29</a> records Paul nearly lynched
          on the accusation that he had brought Trophimus the Ephesian past it. And then
          Ephesians 2:18: "through him we both have <strong>access</strong>
          (<em>prosagoge</em>) in one Spirit to the Father." The access word appears three times
          in Paul (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 5:2">Rom 5:2</a>; Eph 2:18; 3:12), and each
          time it is the settled, granted version of what Korah tried to take.
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Pattern in Acts</h3>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Acts 8</strong> — the Ethiopian eunuch, excluded by Deuteronomy 23:1,
          promised by Isaiah 56, baptised on the road. <em>"What prevents me?"</em></li>
          <li><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 10">Acts 10</a>–11</strong> — Cornelius.
          Peter's "God has shown me that I should not call any person common or unclean"; the
          Spirit falls on Gentiles before baptism, settling the question by act rather than
          argument. Peter's defence in ch. 11 is simply a narration of what God did — "who was I
          that I could stand in God's way?"</li>
          <li><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 15">Acts 15</a></strong> — the Jerusalem
          council, formalising it. Note the method: testimony of what God has done, then
          Scripture (Amos 9), then a decision framed as a minimal burden.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Each step widens the <em>qahal</em> by removing an entry condition. The trajectory
        ends at <a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 7:9">Revelation 7:9</a> — a great multitude
        from every nation, tribe, people, and language, standing before the throne.</p>

        <p><strong>Romans 15:16</strong> completes the logic. Paul's evangelism is
        <em>hierourgeo</em> — priestly service — and the Gentiles he brings are the
        <em>prosphora</em>, the offering. The people once barred from the sanctuary are now what
        gets carried into it. <em>The censer of Numbers 16 held incense that killed the men who
        lifted it. The offering of Romans 15 is people, and it is acceptable.</em></p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="message">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Salvation Message — What Is Actually Preached</h2>

        <p>Strip the sermons in Acts (2:22–39; 3:12–26; 10:34–43; 13:16–41; 17:22–31) and the
        summary in <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 15:3-5">1 Corinthians 15:3–5</a> to
        their recurring elements, and a consistent structure emerges:</p>

        <ol>
          <li><strong>God has acted in fulfilment of promise</strong> — the story has a history,
          and the hearer is inside it.</li>
          <li><strong>Jesus of Nazareth</strong>: attested by God, crucified by human hands,
          raised on the third day.</li>
          <li><strong>According to the Scriptures</strong>, and attested by witnesses.</li>
          <li><strong>Exalted</strong> — Lord and Christ, appointed judge.</li>
          <li><strong>Therefore repent and believe</strong> — receive forgiveness of sins and
          the gift of the Holy Spirit.</li>
          <li><strong>The offer is open</strong> — "the promise is for you and for your children
          and for all who are far off" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 2:39">Acts 2:39</a>).</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">What Is Conspicuously Absent</span>
          <p>The apostolic preaching never argues from the hearer's existing holiness. It never
          says <em>you are worthy, therefore approach.</em> That is Korah's sentence, and it is
          not in any sermon in Acts.</p>
          <p>Every apostolic appeal grounds access in something <strong>outside the hearer and
          already finished</strong>: he was delivered up for our trespasses and raised for our
          justification (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 4:25">Romans 4:25</a>); while we were
          still weak, at the right time, Christ died for the ungodly
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 5:6">Romans 5:6</a>); God shows his love in that
          while we were still sinners, Christ died for us (Romans 5:8).</p>
          <p><a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 3:21-26">Romans 3:21–26</a> states the mechanism:
          righteousness apart from law, through faith, for all who believe — justified by his
          grace as a gift, through the redemption in Christ Jesus, whom God put forward as a
          <em>hilasterion</em>. <strong>That last word is the mercy seat, the lid of the ark, the
          one place in the tabernacle where blood was applied on the Day of Atonement. Paul
          answers the access question by naming the very furniture Korah was trying to reach —
          and says it is now publicly displayed.</strong></p>
        </div>

        <p>Then <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 5:1-2">Romans 5:1–2</a>: "since we have been
        justified by faith, we have peace with God through our Lord Jesus Christ, through whom
        we have obtained <strong>access</strong> by faith into this grace in which we stand."</p>

        <p><a class="ref" data-ref="2 Corinthians 5:18-21">2 Corinthians 5:18–21</a>: God has
        given <em>us</em> the ministry of reconciliation and entrusted to us the message of
        reconciliation; "therefore we are ambassadors for Christ, God making his appeal through
        us." This is the priesthood of all believers in its evangelistic mode.
        <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 2:9">1 Peter 2:9</a> states the purpose clause
        explicitly: a royal priesthood "that you may proclaim the excellencies of him who called
        you out of darkness into his marvellous light."</p>

        <div class="tg-definition">
          <span class="tg-definition__term">The shape of the gospel, stated against Korah</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__def"><em>Not</em> — you are holy, therefore you may draw
          near. <strong>But</strong> — you were far off; Christ has brought you near by his blood
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 2:13">Ephesians 2:13</a>); therefore draw near, and
          go tell the others who are far off.</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="works">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Obedient Works — The Structure</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Ordering</h3>

        <p>The New Testament is emphatic and consistent about the <em>sequence</em>, and nearly
        every dispute about works comes from disturbing it.
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 2:8-10">Ephesians 2:8–10</a> states it in one breath:
        <em>by grace you have been saved through faith, and this is not your own doing; it is the
        gift of God, not a result of works, so that no one may boast</em> — <strong>and then</strong>
        — <em>for we are his workmanship, created in Christ Jesus for good works, which God
        prepared beforehand, that we should walk in them.</em></p>

        <p>Works are the <em>destination</em>, not the entry fee. The same structure appears in
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Titus 2:11-14">Titus 2:11–14</a> and
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Titus 3:4-8">Titus 3:4–8</a>.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Reality of the Demand</h3>

        <p>An honest treatment cannot stop at the ordering, because the New Testament's
        insistence on actual obedience is severe:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="James 2:14-26">James 2:14–26</a></strong> — faith
          without works is dead; even the demons believe, and shudder. The standard
          harmonisation (different opponents, and <em>dikaioo</em> used in the sense of
          vindication rather than initial acquittal) is defensible and held across traditions,
          but it should not be used to make James say less than he says.</li>
          <li><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 7:21-23">Matthew 7:21–23</a></strong> —
          "Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven." And the
          verdict on those who prophesied and cast out demons in his name: <strong>"I never knew
          you."</strong></li>
          <li><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 25:31-46">Matthew 25:31–46</a></strong> —
          the sheep and goats, sorted by what was done to the hungry, the stranger, the sick, the
          imprisoned; and neither party knew what it had been doing.</li>
          <li><strong>Hebrews 6:4–8; 10:26–31</strong> — the warning passages, in the same letter
          that opened the sanctuary.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Exact Negative of the Korah Verse</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Numbers 16:5, quoted at 2 Timothy 2:19: <em>the Lord
          knows those who are his.</em> Matthew 7:23: <em>I never knew you.</em></p>
          <p>Both texts locate the decisive fact in God's knowledge rather than in the claimant's
          assertion — and Matthew's examples are people with <em>impressive spiritual
          credentials</em>, which is precisely the profile of the 250: "chieftains of the
          congregation, chosen from the assembly, men of renown."</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Engine</h3>

        <p>What keeps the demand from becoming a new law is the New Testament's account of
        <em>how</em> obedience is produced: <strong>union with Christ</strong>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 6">Romans 6</a> — obedience is not the price of the
        union; it is what the union does); <strong>the Spirit</strong>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 5:16-25">Galatians 5:16–25</a> — the fruit is fruit,
        not manufacture); <strong>abiding</strong>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 15:4-5">John 15:4–5</a> — apart from me you can do
        nothing); and <strong>faith working through love</strong>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 5:6">Galatians 5:6</a>).</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Works Tested by Fire</h3>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">The Same Ordeal, With the Person Spared</span>
          <p><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 3:10-15">1 Corinthians 3:10–15</a> is the New
          Testament passage closest in mechanism to the censer ordeal, and the parallel is exact
          enough to be worth noticing.</p>
          <p>In Numbers 16, the dispute over legitimate ministry is submitted to a
          <strong>fire test</strong>: each man takes his censer, and God discloses whose offering
          he accepts. The fire came, and it consumed the men.</p>
          <p>In 1 Corinthians 3, each one's work in building the church is submitted to a fire
          test: "the Day will disclose it, because it will be revealed by fire, and the fire will
          test what sort of work each one has done." The fire comes, and it consumes the
          <strong>work</strong> — "if anyone's work is burned up, he will suffer loss, though he
          himself will be saved, but only as through fire."</p>
          <p>This is not incidental: the immediately preceding verses are about the Corinthian
          party spirit, the Korah-shaped conflict of the letter, and Paul's answer is that
          ministers are servants, that God gives the growth, and that judgment on ministry belongs
          to the Day. Then 4:5 — "do not pronounce judgment before the time."</p>
        </div>

        <p><a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 12:28-29">Hebrews 12:28–29</a> keeps the older
        register deliberately: <em>let us offer to God acceptable worship, with reverence and awe,
        for our God is a consuming fire.</em> The access is total; the God being accessed has not
        changed.</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">The Residue</span>
          Part III mapped where Catholic and Protestant accounts still differ on merit and on the
          imputation/impartation distinction, and this part does not dissolve it. What can be said
          is that the <em>sequence</em> is not in dispute: the Second Council of Orange condemned
          semi-Pelagianism; Trent opens from prevenient grace; CCC 2010 denies that anyone merits
          initial grace; every Protestant confession affirms that the faith which justifies is
          never alone. <strong>Both traditions place grace first and works after, and both insist
          the works are real.</strong> The dispute concerns what the works then are in relation to
          final salvation, not whether they are required.
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="leadership">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Leadership Reordered</h2>

        <p>The New Testament handles the status question directly, and it does so —
        significantly — without ever reaching for Numbers 16.</p>

        <p><a class="ref" data-ref="Mark 10:35-45">Mark 10:35–45</a> is the closest thing in the
        Gospels to a Korah scenario: James and John request the two highest places, the other ten
        are indignant, and a status conflict breaks out inside the leadership. Jesus does not
        judge them. He teaches:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          You know that those who are considered rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them, and
          their great ones exercise authority over them. But it shall not be so among you.
          Whoever would be great among you must be your servant… For even the Son of Man came not
          to be served but to serve, and to give his life as a ransom for many.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Mark 10:42-45">Mark 10:42–45</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Three things follow. <strong>First</strong>, the desire for greatness is not forbidden
        — it is redirected, and the way up is redefined. <strong>Second</strong>, the Gentile
        pattern of authority is named and excluded, which means the New Testament anticipates that
        Christian leadership will be tempted toward exactly that pattern. <strong>Third</strong>,
        the model is the cross, and it is enacted the same week in the footwashing
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 13:1-17">John 13:1–17</a>).</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Disclaimer of Gain</h3>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead><tr><th>Speaker</th><th>Setting</th><th>Words</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Moses</strong></td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 16:15">Num 16:15</a>, under Korah's accusation</td><td>"I have not taken one donkey from them, and I have not harmed one of them"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Samuel</strong></td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Samuel 12:3">1 Sam 12:3</a>, laying down leadership</td><td>"Whose ox have I taken? Or whose donkey have I taken?… Testify against me"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Paul</strong></td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 20:33-35">Acts 20:33–35</a>, farewell at Miletus</td><td>"I coveted no one's silver or gold or apparel… these hands ministered to my necessities"</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Three leaders, at moments when their authority is in question or being handed on,
        submit their financial conduct to public audit. This is the New Testament's inherited
        standard, and it becomes an ordination requirement: an overseer must be "not a lover of
        money" (1 Timothy 3:3), and elders must serve "not for shameful gain, but eagerly"
        (1 Peter 5:2).</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Weakness as Credential</h3>

        <p><a class="ref" data-ref="2 Corinthians 10">2 Corinthians 10</a>–13 is Paul's response
        to rival "super-apostles" who evidently outperformed him in presence, rhetoric, and
        self-commendation. His answer is the boast in weakness: the catalogue of beatings,
        shipwrecks, and sleepless nights (11:23–28), the thorn, and "my grace is sufficient for
        you, for my power is made perfect in weakness" (12:9).</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Résumé Runs the Other Way</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Korah's coalition was made of "chieftains of the
          congregation, chosen from the assembly, men of renown" — a résumé of exactly the kind
          the super-apostles presented. Paul's counter-argument is that in the church the résumé
          runs the other way.</p>
          <p><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 4:1-5">1 Corinthians 4:1–5</a> supplies the
          posture: ministers are "servants of Christ and stewards of the mysteries of God"; Paul
          does not even judge himself; "it is the Lord who judges me." The one who most obviously
          could have pulled rank in the New Testament repeatedly declines to — which is the same
          behaviour Numbers 16 attributes to Moses.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="mixed">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Mixed Body and the Final Sorting</h2>

        <ul>
          <li><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 13:24-30">Matthew 13:24–30</a></strong>,
          36–43, 47–50 — the wheat and the tares, the dragnet. The servants want to pull the
          weeds; the master forbids it, <em>lest in gathering the weeds you root up the wheat
          along with them.</em> The sorting is at the harvest, by angels. This is the definitive
          dominical statement on premature separation, and it stands in permanent tension with the
          separatist reading of Numbers 16:26.</li>
          <li><strong>2 Timothy 2:19–21</strong> — the great house, vessels of honour and
          dishonour. The seal has two sides: <em>the Lord knows those who are his</em>, and
          <em>let everyone who names the name of the Lord depart from iniquity.</em> God's
          knowledge, and my repentance — not my sorting of others.</li>
          <li><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 2:19">1 John 2:19</a></strong> — "they went
          out from us, but they were not of us." A retrospective diagnosis, made after the fact,
          not a tool for identifying who will leave.</li>
          <li><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 5">1 Corinthians 5</a></strong> —
          and yet: the church does exercise discipline in cases of unrepented, public, grave sin.
          The tares parable forbids attempted sorting of the invisible; it does not forbid the
          visible discipline the epistles require. Holding both is the whole practical difficulty
          of church life.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Where Jude Ends</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The Korah letter ends in a rescue operation.</p>
          <p>Jude 11 is the verse that licensed two thousand years of Korah polemic. After the
          harshest denunciation in the New Testament — Cain, Balaam, Korah; waterless clouds;
          twice dead, uprooted; wandering stars — Jude turns to his readers and writes:
          <em>"Have mercy on those who doubt; save others by snatching them out of the fire; to
          others show mercy with fear"</em>
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Jude 22-23">Jude 22–23</a>).</p>
          <p>Its final instruction about people in danger of the fire is to reach in and pull them
          out. And its closing doxology is to the one "who is able to keep you from stumbling" —
          that is, the readers are told in the same breath that they are not themselves secure by
          their own competence. <strong>Anyone who cites Jude 11 and does not cite Jude 22–23 has
          quoted half a sentence.</strong></p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Where It All Ends</h3>

        <p><a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 21:22">Revelation 21:22</a>: "And I saw no temple
        in the city, for its temple is the Lord God the Almighty and the Lamb." There is no
        sanctuary because there is no longer any restricted zone. And
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 22:4">Revelation 22:4</a>, of the servants of God:
        "they will see his face." <a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 33:20">Exodus 33:20</a> — "you
        cannot see my face, for man shall not see me and live."</p>

        <p><em>Korah asked whether an ordinary Israelite could come near the holy place. The
        canon's last chapter answers that the redeemed will see the face that Moses was not
        permitted to see.</em></p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="synthesis">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Synthesis — Korah's Question, the New Testament's Answer</h2>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Seven Findings</span>
          <p><strong>1. The boundary is removed — on another basis.</strong> Korah's conclusion
          is granted at Hebrews 10:19–22 and Ephesians 2:18; his ground is denied. The mechanism
          is the blood of Jesus, and it is entirely outside the worshipper.</p>
          <p><strong>2. The priesthood is universal — as a gift.</strong> 1 Peter 2:5, 9 repeats
          Korah's proof-text and adds <em>through Jesus Christ</em>. Moses had already wanted this
          (Numbers 11:29); Pentecost gives it to sons, daughters, and slaves.</p>
          <p><strong>3. Office remains — and is reordered.</strong> No one takes the honour to
          himself (Hebrews 5:4), and no one lords it over the flock (Mark 10:43; 1 Peter 5:3). The
          qualifications are character; the credential is weakness; the finances are auditable.</p>
          <p><strong>4. Dispute has a procedure.</strong> Matthew 18 requires the private approach
          first and aims at restoration. Acts 6 shows a grievance against leadership conceded and
          remedied. Galatians 2 shows an apostle publicly corrected. None of this is Korahite; the
          difference is manner and object.</p>
          <p><strong>5. The direction is outward.</strong> The Sinai economy guarded a centre; the
          apostolic economy sends from an opened one. The evangelist is a priest and the nations
          are the offering (Romans 15:16).</p>
          <p><strong>6. Works follow, and they are real.</strong> Created <em>for</em> good works
          (Ephesians 2:10), not saved by them; tested by fire that consumes the work and spares the
          worker (1 Corinthians 3:15); and sorted at the end by a knowledge that is God's, not
          ours.</p>
          <p><strong>7. The sorting is deferred.</strong> Wheat and tares stand together until the
          harvest. Do not pronounce judgment before the time. And when someone is in the fire, the
          instruction is to snatch them out.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-application">
          <span class="tg-application__label">Six Ways to Use This With a Congregation or Class</span>
          <p><strong>1. Teach Numbers 11:29 before Numbers 16.</strong> Moses wanted what Korah
          wanted. Nothing else clarifies the passage so quickly, and it protects hearers from
          concluding that desiring more of God is the sin.</p>
          <p><strong>2. Land every Korah study on Hebrews 10:19–25.</strong> Draw near — hold fast
          — stir one another up. The vertical answer and the horizontal consequence in the same
          paragraph.</p>
          <p><strong>3. Use Acts 6 as the counter-case.</strong> The single most useful passage for
          congregations that have been taught to fear their own questions.</p>
          <p><strong>4. Preach the fire twice.</strong> Numbers 16:35 consumed the men;
          1 Corinthians 3:15 consumes the work and saves the man. The change between them is the
          gospel.</p>
          <p><strong>5. Read Jude to the end.</strong> Verse 11 and verses 22–23 belong in the same
          sermon or neither.</p>
          <p><strong>6. Finish at Revelation 21:22 and 22:4.</strong> No temple, and they will see
          his face. The access question closed in the affirmative, forever.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div class="tg-reading">
        <h2 class="tg-reading__title">For Further Study</h2>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>The Temple and the Church's Mission</cite>, G. K. Beale. IVP, 2004.
          <em>The fullest treatment of how temple language migrates from a building to a people —
          directly relevant to what happens to Korah's question.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Images of the Church in the New Testament</cite>, Paul Minear. Westminster, 1960.
          <em>The standard catalogue; useful precisely for what it does not find.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Lay People in the Church</cite>, Yves Congar OP. Newman Press, 1957.
          <em>The most serious Catholic treatment of the common priesthood, and the theological
          groundwork for Vatican II's account of the laity.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Being as Communion</cite>, John Zizioulas. St Vladimir's, 1985.
          <em>The Orthodox account of church as communion rather than institution — the third
          party's contribution to this question.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>The Mission of God</cite>, Christopher J. H. Wright. IVP, 2006.
          <em>The best single volume on the canon's outward trajectory from Abraham to
          Revelation 7.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>The Gospel in a Pluralist Society</cite>, Lesslie Newbigin. Eerdmans, 1989.
          <em>On the church as sign and instrument rather than fortress.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Paul and the Gift</cite>, John Barclay. Eerdmans, 2015.
          <em>The most important recent treatment of grace and its logic; decisive for the
          works-sequence question.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Life Together</cite>, Dietrich Bonhoeffer. 1939.
          <em>Short, and the best practical exposition of what the "one another" commands
          actually require.</em>
        </p>
